    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#tacitus"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tacitus.jpg" alt="Tacitus" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#tacitus">Tacitus</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wien-_Parlament-Tacitus.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Pe-Jo, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#svear">Svear</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#lake-malaren">Mälaren</a> valley and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#uppland">Uppland</a>, centred on the royal and cult site of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#uppsala">Uppsala</a>, appear in sources from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tacitus">Tacitus</a> onward, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#geats">Geats</a> lived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#vastergotland">Västergötland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ostergotland">Östergötland</a> to the south-west. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#viking-age">Viking Age</a>, Swedish traders and warriors, often called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#varangians">Varangians</a> or Rus, travelled the rivers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#russia">Russia</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#caliphate">Caliphate</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#birka">Birka</a> on Lake Mälaren was a major trading town. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#eric-the-victorious">Eric the Victorious</a>, who according to saga tradition won the Battle of Fyrisvellir near Uppsala around 985, is the first king whose rule over the Svear is reasonably certain. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#olof-skotkonung">Olof Skötkonung</a> (r. c. 995–c. 1022), the first Christian king, was probably the first to rule both Svear and Geats, and he struck the first Swedish coins at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sigtuna">Sigtuna</a>.</p><p>Christianisation was slow: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#adam-of-bremen">Adam of Bremen</a> described a pagan temple at Uppsala in the 1070s, and the archbishopric of Uppsala was founded only in 1164. For two centuries the rival houses of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#house-of-sverker">Sverker</a> and Eric alternated on the throne. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#birger-jarl">Birger Jarl</a>, who governed for his son King Valdemar from 1250 until 1266, strengthened the crown, issued peace laws, founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#stockholm">Stockholm</a>, first mentioned in 1252, and led an expedition, traditionally dated to 1249, that brought Tavastia in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#finland">Finland</a> under Swedish rule.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#stockholm"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/stockholm.jpg" alt="Stockholm" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#stockholm">Stockholm</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Royal_Dramatic_Theatre_Stockholm.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Steven Lek, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sweden">Sweden</a> was a thinly populated land of peasant farmers who, unlike their counterparts in much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#europe">Europe</a>, were never reduced to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#serfdom">serfdom</a> and later formed their own estate in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#riksdag">Riksdag</a>. Mining shaped the economy from the thirteenth century: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#copper">copper</a> mountain of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#falun-mine">Falun</a> and the iron districts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bergslagen">Bergslagen</a> drew German capital and miners, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#stockholm">Stockholm</a> grew as a port with a strong Hanseatic presence. Copper financed the wars of the seventeenth century, when Sweden was Europe’s leading producer, and bar iron became the main export of the eighteenth. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#gustav-i-of-sweden">Gustav Vasa</a>’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#riksdag-of-vasteras-1527">Riksdag of Västerås</a> in 1527 began the transfer of church wealth to the crown and the making of a Lutheran state, and church examinations of reading made the population unusually literate by the eighteenth century.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sveriges-riksbank">Riksbank</a>, founded in 1668, is the oldest central bank still operating, and its predecessor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#stockholms-banco">Stockholms Banco</a> issued Europe’s first banknotes in 1661. In the nineteenth and early twentieth centuries over a million Swedes emigrated, mostly to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#united-states">United States</a>, before industrialisation based on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#timber">timber</a>, iron ore, engineering and hydroelectric power made Sweden rich. From the 1930s the Social Democrats built the <em>folkhem</em>, or “people’s home”, a comprehensive welfare state resting on agreements between employers and unions such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#saltsjobaden-agreement">Saltsjöbaden Agreement</a> of 1938.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-brunkeberg"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-brunkeberg.jpg" alt="Battle of Brunkeberg" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-brunkeberg">Battle of Brunkeberg</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Entry_of_Sten_Sture_the_Elder_into_Stockholm_(Georg_von_Rosen)_-_Nationalmuseum_-_19214.tif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Georg von Rosen, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Medieval Swedish warfare was directed east: expeditions traditionally called the Swedish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#crusade">crusades</a> extended rule over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#finland">Finland</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#torgils-knutsson">Torgils Knutsson</a> founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#vyborg">Vyborg</a> castle in 1293, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#treaty-of-noteborg">Treaty of Nöteborg</a> of 1323 fixed a first border with Novgorod. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kalmar-union">Kalmar Union</a>, peasant armies led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#engelbrekt-engelbrektsson">Engelbrekt Engelbrektsson</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sture-family">Sture</a> regents resisted Danish kings, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sten-sture-the-elder">Sten Sture the Elder</a> defeated Christian I at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-brunkeberg">Battle of Brunkeberg</a> in 1471. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#gustav-i-of-sweden">Gustav Vasa</a>’s war of liberation (1521–1523) created the independent kingdom, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#eric-xiv-of-sweden">Eric XIV</a> took Reval and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#estonia">Estonia</a> in 1561.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#gustavus-adolphus">Gustav II Adolf</a> built an army of conscripted peasant soldiers with mobile <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#field-artillery">field artillery</a> and flexible tactics. He won <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ingria">Ingria</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#russia">Russia</a> by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#treaty-of-stolbovo">Treaty of Stolbovo</a> in 1617, took Riga in 1621 and in 1630 entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#thirty-tyrants">the Thirty</a> Years’ War, winning the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-breitenfeld-1631">Battle of Breitenfeld</a> in 1631 before he was killed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-lutzen-1632">Lützen</a> in 1632. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#charles-x-gustav">Charles X Gustav</a> crossed the frozen belts to force the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#treaty-of-roskilde">Treaty of Roskilde</a> on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#denmark">Denmark</a> in 1658, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#charles-xi-of-sweden">Charles XI</a>’s allotment system (<em>indelningsverket</em>) settled soldiers on farms that maintained them. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#charles-xii-of-sweden">Charles XII</a> won at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#narva">Narva</a> in 1700 but was crushed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#peter-the-great">Peter the Great</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-poltava">Battle of Poltava</a> in 1709 and killed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#siege-of-fredriksten">Fredriksten</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#norway">Norway</a> in 1718. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sweden">Sweden</a>’s last war was a brief campaign against Norway in 1814.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#ordinance-of-alsno"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ordinance-of-alsno.jpg" alt="Ordinance of Alsnö" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#ordinance-of-alsno">Ordinance of Alsnö</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Magnus_Ladul%C3%A5s_patronus.Riddarholmskyrka_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Alexandru Baboş Albabos, CC BY 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Medieval kings were elected at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#stones-of-mora">Stones of Mora</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#uppsala">Uppsala</a> and ruled with a council of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#magnates">magnates</a>. Magnus Ladulås’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ordinance-of-alsno">Ordinance of Alsnö</a> (c. 1280) created a tax-exempt nobility in return for mounted service, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#magnus-eriksson">Magnus Eriksson</a>, king of both <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sweden">Sweden</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#norway">Norway</a> from 1319, issued a national Land Law around 1350 to replace the provincial codes. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#riksdag">Riksdag</a> grew from fifteenth-century assemblies, conventionally traced to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#arboga">Arboga</a> in 1435, into a parliament of four estates: nobles, clergy, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#burghers">burghers</a> and peasants. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#gustav-i-of-sweden">Gustav Vasa</a>, elected in 1523, made the crown hereditary by the Succession Pact of Västerås in 1544. The chancellor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#axel-oxenstierna">Axel Oxenstierna</a> gave the state its collegiate central government in the Instrument of Government of 1634, with counties under governors.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#charles-xi-of-sweden">Charles XI</a> made the crown absolute in 1680 and carried out the Reduction, recovering crown lands from the nobility. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#charles-xii-of-sweden">Charles XII</a>’s death the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#age-of-liberty">Age of Liberty</a> (1719–1772) gave power to the Riksdag and its parties, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#hats-party">Hats</a> and the Caps, until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#gustav-iii">Gustav III</a>’s coup of 1772. The Instrument of Government of 1809 restored <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#constitutional-monarchy">constitutional monarchy</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#charles-xiv-john">Jean-Baptiste Bernadotte</a>, elected crown prince in 1810, became Charles XIV John in 1818. Parliamentary government was accepted in 1917, and the Instrument of Government of 1974 left the monarch a purely ceremonial role.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#systema-naturae"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/systema-naturae.jpg" alt="Systema Naturae" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#systema-naturae">Systema Naturae</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Linnaeus1758-title-page.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Carl Linnaeus, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Swedish history shaped northern and eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#europe">Europe</a>. Viking-age Swedes helped open the trade routes of the Rus, and medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sweden">Sweden</a> incorporated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#finland">Finland</a>, which for six centuries shared Swedish law, Lutheran religion and administration; Swedish remains an official language of Finland. In the seventeenth century Swedish intervention rescued the Protestant cause in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#thirty-tyrants">the Thirty</a> Years’ War, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#peace-of-westphalia">Peace of Westphalia</a> of 1648 gave Sweden <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#pomerania">Western Pomerania</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bremen-verden">Bremen-Verden</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#wismar">Wismar</a> and a voice in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>. Its Baltic empire reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#livonia">Livonia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ingria">Ingria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#estonia">Estonia</a>, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#gustavus-adolphus">Gustav II Adolf</a> founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#university-of-tartu">University of Dorpat</a> (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tartu">Tartu</a>) in 1632. Sweden also held New Sweden on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#delaware-river">Delaware River</a> from 1638 to 1655 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#saint-barthelemy">Saint Barthélemy</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#caribbean">Caribbean</a> from 1784 to 1878.</p><p>Swedish scientists included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#carl-linnaeus">Carl Linnaeus</a>, whose <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#systema-naturae">Systema Naturae</a></em> (1735) founded modern biological classification, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#anders-celsius">Anders Celsius</a>, and the will of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#alfred-nobel">Alfred Nobel</a> created the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#nobel-prize">Nobel Prizes</a>, first awarded in 1901. In the twentieth century Sweden’s welfare model and diplomats such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#raoul-wallenberg">Raoul Wallenberg</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#dag-hammarskjold">Dag Hammarskjöld</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#secretary-general-of-the-united-nations">Secretary-General of the United Nations</a> from 1953 to 1961, gave it an international standing out of proportion to its size.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Into the Present</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#finnish-war"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/finnish-war.jpg" alt="Finnish War" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#finnish-war">Finnish War</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Finnish_War_Collage_5.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Imonoz, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Poltava and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#treaty-of-nystad">Treaty of Nystad</a> of 1721 ended <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sweden">Sweden</a>’s great-power era, transferring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#livonia">Livonia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#estonia">Estonia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ingria">Ingria</a> and part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#karelia">Karelia</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#russia">Russia</a>. Wars against Russia in 1741–1743 and 1788–1790 failed to reverse the verdict, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#finnish-war">Finnish War</a> of 1808–1809 Russia conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#finland">Finland</a>, ceded by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#treaty-of-fredrikshamn">Treaty of Fredrikshamn</a> in 1809; the defeat led to the deposition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#gustav-iv-adolf">Gustav IV Adolf</a>. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#charles-xiv-john">Bernadotte</a>, who joined the coalition against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#napoleon">Napoleon</a>, Sweden gained <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#norway">Norway</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#denmark">Denmark</a> by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#treaty-of-kiel">Treaty of Kiel</a> in 1814 and forced it into a personal union, which lasted until Norway’s peaceful secession in 1905. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#pomerania">Swedish Pomerania</a> was given up in 1815.</p><p>Thereafter Sweden kept out of war. It stayed neutral in both world wars, although it sold iron ore to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#germany">Germany</a> and allowed German troop transit in 1940–1943, while also sheltering Danish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#jews">Jews</a> and refugees from Norway and the Baltic. After 1945 it pursued armed neutrality and an active role in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#united-nations">United Nations</a>. Sweden joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#european-union">European Union</a> in 1995 but kept its own currency after rejecting the euro in 2003. After Russia’s full-scale invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ukraine">Ukraine</a> it abandoned two centuries of non-alignment and joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#nato">NATO</a> in March 2024. It remains a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#constitutional-monarchy">constitutional monarchy</a> under King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#carl-xvi-gustaf">Carl XVI Gustaf</a>.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sweden">Sweden</a> matters as a kingdom that grew from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#svear">Svear</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#geats">Geats</a> into the dominant Baltic power of the seventeenth century, shaping <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#thirty-tyrants">the Thirty</a> Years’ War and the eastern Baltic, and then remade itself through neutrality, industrialisation and social democracy into one of the world’s most prosperous and stable states.</em></p>

